[56.220, "o", "# 0 Scan Report\r\n\r\n| Field | Value |\r\n|-------|-------|\r\n| Target | source:app |\r\n| Started | 2026-11-24T06:30:19.335Z |\r\n| Duration | 58.0s |\r\n\r\n## Summary\r\n\r\n- **Findings:** 3\r\n\r\n### Technical severity\r\n\r\n- **Critical:** 1\r\n- **High:** 2\r\n\r\n## Files opened\r\n\r\nThe scan opened 3 of 3 source files.\r\n\r\n## Findings\r\n\r\n### Arbitrary JavaScript execution through price formula\r\n\r\n- **What an attacker can do:** A caller who passes the global token gate can execute arbitrary JavaScript in the API process, including invoking child processes with the service account's privileges; exact host access and deployment reachability are unknown.\r\n- **Who can do it:** any logged-in user\r\n- **What they get:** run their own code\r\n- **Technical severity:** critical\r\n- **Category:** code-injection\r\n- **Status:** confirmed\r\n- **Confidence:** 75%\r\n- **Description:** `GET /price` passes the untrusted `formula` query parameter directly to `eval`. A caller passing the global API-token middleware can execute JavaScript in the server proc"]
[0.000, "o", "ess; for example, `require('child_process').execSync('id').toString()` is evaluated in the CommonJS module scope and the returned string is sent as JSON. The token is hardcoded at server.js:7, but actual deployment reachability is unknown.\r\n\r\n**Evidence:** CWE: CWE-94\r\n\r\nSource-verified: the route supplies attacker-controlled text to JavaScript eval and returns the evaluation result. In this CommonJS source, `require` is in module scope and accessible to direct eval, so the example can invoke a child process and return its output. No server was started; impact outside the process depends on its runtime privileges and deployment.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Request / Response</summary>\r\n\r\n**Request:**\r\n```\r\n`GET /price?formula=require%28%27child_process%27%29.execSync%28%27id%27%29.toString%28%29` with the accepted `x-api-token` header.\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Response:**\r\n```\r\n`server.js:24` evaluates `req.query.formula` with `eval`; the result is placed in `total` and returned through `res.json({ total })` at line 25.\r\n```\r\n</"]
[0.000, "o", "details>\r\n\r\n### Hardcoded API authentication token\r\n\r\n- **What an attacker can do:** A caller who obtains the source can use the embedded token to pass the application's only request gate and reach both API routes. No deployed environment or data exposure is established by this code review.\r\n- **Who can do it:** any logged-in user\r\n- **What they get:** change data they should not be able to change\r\n- **Technical severity:** high\r\n- **Category:** hardcoded-secret\r\n- **Status:** confirmed\r\n- **Confidence:** 80%\r\n- **Description:** The sole request gate compares `x-api-token` directly to a fixed literal in source. Anyone with access to this source can recover the credential and authenticate to every route; rotation also requires a code change. Deployment exposure and source visibility are not established here.\r\n\r\n**Evidence:** CWE: CWE-798\r\n\r\nSource-verified: the literal is the credential that gates all routes. The request shown passes the gate as written. This finding does not establish that the service is depl"]
[0.000, "o", "oyed or that its source is externally available; the credential is nevertheless committed in code and is usable by anyone who can read it.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Request / Response</summary>\r\n\r\n**Request:**\r\n```\r\nHTTP request with header `x-api-token: k3Jx9Qw2LpZ8vR5tYb7NcM4dHs6FgA1e`.\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Response:**\r\n```\r\n`server.js:7` defines `const API_TOKEN = 'k3Jx9Qw2LpZ8vR5tYb7NcM4dHs6FgA1e';`; `server.js:9-12` calls `next()` when the request header equals that literal, otherwise returns 401.\r\n```\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### SQL injection in customer order lookup\r\n\r\n- **What an attacker can do:** A caller who passes the API-token middleware can alter the customer filter and request order rows outside the chosen customer; the endpoint returns matching rows to the caller. The actual sensitivity and volume of database contents are unknown.\r\n- **Who can do it:** any logged-in user\r\n- **What they get:** read data they should not be able to read\r\n- **Technical severity:** high\r\n- **Category:** sql-injection\r\n- **Status:** confi"]
[0.000, "o", "rmed\r\n- **Confidence:** 78%\r\n- **Description:** `GET /orders` concatenates the untrusted `customer` query parameter into a SQLite SQL string. A caller who passes the global token gate can change the WHERE clause, for example with `customer=' OR 1=1 --`, and retrieve rows beyond the intended customer filter. The route then serializes those rows in its response. The token is hardcoded at server.js:7, but actual deployment reachability is unknown.\r\n\r\n**Evidence:** CWE: CWE-89\r\n\r\nSource-verified: the query parameter is interpolated into SQL syntax with no escaping or parameter binding, and `.all()` returns the resulting rows. The example changes the predicate to a tautology in SQLite. Database contents and live execution were not available for verification.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Request / Response</summary>\r\n\r\n**Request:**\r\n```\r\n`GET /orders?customer=%27%20OR%201%3D1%20--` with the accepted `x-api-token` header.\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Response:**\r\n```\r\n`server.js:17` builds `SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer = '` + `req.qu"]
[0.000, "o", "ery.customer` + `'`; line 18 executes it with `.all()`, and lines 19-20 return all resulting rows as JSON via `formatOrder`.\r\n```\r\n</details>\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "opened 3 of 3 source files\r\n"]
